1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:03,100
Shields up.

2
00:00:03,100 --> 00:00:04,200
Come about, now.

3
00:00:04,200 --> 00:00:05,300
He's closing fast.

4
00:00:05,300 --> 00:00:06,400
Report status.

5
00:00:06,400 --> 00:00:07,500
No answer yet.

6
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:08,600
We're hit!

7
00:00:08,600 --> 00:00:09,700
Hold steady.

8
00:00:09,700 --> 00:00:10,800
Fire at will.

9
00:00:10,800 --> 00:00:11,900
It's a trap.

10
00:00:11,900 --> 00:00:13,000
Fall back now.

11
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:14,100
Systems online.

12
00:00:14,100 --> 00:00:15,200
Copy that.

13
00:00:15,200 --> 00:00:16,300
Stand by.

14
00:00:16,300 --> 00:00:17,400
Go, go, go.

15
00:00:17,400 --> 00:00:18,500
They're gone.

16
00:00:18,500 --> 00:00:19,600
Clear the deck.

17
00:00:19,900 --> 00:00:22,700
The diagnostics keep reporting the same intermittent fault in the port stabiliser.

18
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:25,400
Long-range sensors show a debris field where the relay station used to be, Captain.

19
00:00:25,700 --> 00:00:28,500
If we re-route power through the auxiliary conduits we might buy ourselves ten minutes.

20
00:00:28,800 --> 00:00:31,200
Their weapons are charged, but they have not locked on and they are holding position.

21
00:00:31,500 --> 00:00:34,300
Whatever happened down there, the logs were wiped before anyone thought to look.

22
00:00:34,600 --> 00:00:37,000
I want a full inventory of every escape pod and a head count within the hour.

23
00:00:37,300 --> 00:00:40,100
The hull temperature is climbing faster than the cooling loop can compensate for.

24
00:00:40,400 --> 00:00:42,800
We can take the long way around the nebula, but it adds four days and a lot of fuel.

25
00:00:43,100 --> 00:00:45,900
Tell the crew to seal the lower decks; I will not risk anyone on a guess.

26
00:00:46,200 --> 00:00:48,600
Comms are back, though the signal keeps breaking up whenever we turn the array.

27
00:00:48,900 --> 00:00:51,700
It is quiet out here, which is exactly the part that worries me the most.

28
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:54,400
Run the simulation again, and this time do not optimise for a happy ending.

29
00:00:55,100 --> 00:01:03,100
We made it.

30
00:01:03,500 --> 00:01:11,500
Quiet.

31
00:01:11,900 --> 00:01:19,900
Not yet.

32
00:01:20,300 --> 00:01:28,300
Almost there.

33
00:01:28,700 --> 00:01:36,700
Listen.

34
00:01:37,100 --> 00:01:45,100
Wait.

35
00:01:45,800 --> 00:01:47,500
Every single time we push the reactor past eighty percent the secondary manifold fractures in a way the manual swears is impossible, and I am tired of guessing why.

36
00:01:47,800 --> 00:01:49,500
The council will demand a full accounting of what we did at the colony, and I would rather hand them a clean record than an explanation nobody in this room believes.

37
00:01:49,800 --> 00:01:51,500
There is a difference between a retreat and a rout, and the only thing separating them is whether the people behind you make it back to the ships in time.

38
00:01:51,800 --> 00:01:53,500
I have read the telemetry twice, and the numbers do not describe a malfunction; they describe somebody deliberately venting the atmosphere from the observation deck.

39
00:01:54,100 --> 00:01:55,600
The transmission was not a distress call at all but a complete manifest of every vessel in the system together with their registry numbers, crew complements, current headings, weapon loadouts, and the exact minute each one is scheduled to leave dock, which means somebody on the inside copied the whole fleet schedule onto a relay that was supposed to have been dark for six years and then chose this precise moment to send it straight to us knowing we would have no time to verify a single word of it.

40
00:01:56,200 --> 00:01:58,600
C'est déjà fini — n'est-ce pas ?

41
00:01:58,900 --> 00:02:01,300
Preço, saúde, coração… tudo bem.

42
00:02:01,600 --> 00:02:04,000
«Guillemets» — em—dash and an ellipsis…

43
00:02:04,300 --> 00:02:06,700
Números: 42, 7 %, e #hashtags.

44
00:02:07,300 --> 00:02:10,300
Two contacts, bearing zero three five.

45
00:02:08,100 --> 00:02:11,100
They're not answering our hails.

46
00:02:11,500 --> 00:02:13,700
I keep telling myself that the door at the end of this corridor

47
00:02:13,900 --> 00:02:16,100
is just a door, and that whatever is standing behind it

48
00:02:16,300 --> 00:02:18,500
has been waiting for us to stop being afraid of it.

49
00:02:22,600 --> 00:02:26,800
Engineering reports the starboard coupling is holding, for now, at ninety-one percent.

50
00:02:27,200 --> 00:02:31,400
Navigation wants a decision before the next shift change, one way or the other.

51
00:02:31,800 --> 00:02:36,000
The mess hall has been converted to a triage bay, which tells you how the day is going.

52
00:02:36,400 --> 00:02:40,600
We are running on reserves, and the reserves are running on optimism and cold coffee.

53
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:45,200
Somebody has been siphoning rations from the aft locker, and I intend to find out who.

54
00:02:45,600 --> 00:02:49,800
The captain's log ends mid-sentence, which is not the part that frightens me.

55
00:02:50,200 --> 00:02:54,400
If the storm holds we can make the crossing before the last of the fuel burns off.

56
00:02:54,800 --> 00:02:59,000
Every panel in this corridor is dead, and yet the door at the end still has power.

57
00:02:59,400 --> 00:03:03,600
I have been staring at this schematic for six hours and it still makes no sense.

58
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:08,200
The refugees are aboard, accounted for, and asking more questions than we can answer.

59
00:03:08,600 --> 00:03:12,800
They will expect a report by morning, so somebody had better invent the truth tonight.

60
00:03:13,200 --> 00:03:17,400
Whatever you decide, decide it now, because the window closes in about four minutes.

61
00:03:17,800 --> 00:03:22,000
